# Milestone: v0.10.2-reactive (activated + semantics corrected at v1.0 / M10,
#            design-risks v0.11.4-A)
# Covers: tag.stop() wakes a sleeping strand mid-sleep and terminates its tagged
#         block.  The TAG_STOP unwind runs the `finally` clause (Realm.fin=true)
#         but is NOT absorbed by `catch` — per REVIVAL §S5a, catch absorbs only
#         THROW, never TAG_STOP/CANCEL — so Realm.caught stays false.
# matrix-row: temporal-sleep
#
# History: the original fixture expected `catch` to fire (Realm.caught=true) on
# tag.stop(); that contradicted the locked S5a semantics and so could not be
# activated.  The real behaviour — sleeper woken, block terminated, finally run,
# catch skipped — is asserted here.  The sleeper is woken via tag.stop()'s
# urbi_sched_strand_unblock (it becomes runnable, so the next step dispatches it).
#
# v0.13.3 (refactor-3 SCHED-01/B10): step results updated under the
# single-writer runnable count.  First step: only a not-yet-due sleeper is
# parked — WAKE_AT, not RUNNING (a WAITING strand is no longer counted as
# runnable).  Second step: the t.stop() run line already woke the strand and
# the REPL drain loop drove it to completion (finally runs, strand dies), so
# nothing live remains — QUIESCENT.  The pre-fix RUNNING results were the
# phantom count left by the park/wake cycle (host busy-spin signal).
#
## host: realm st
## host: run var t = Tag.new("sleeper")
## host: run Realm.fin = false
## host: run Realm.caught = false
## host: run t: { try { sleep(500ms) } catch (e) { Realm.caught = true } finally { Realm.fin = true } }
## host: step 2000
## host: run t.stop()
## host: step 2000
## host: run Realm.fin
## host: run Realm.caught
[00000000] <Tag: sleeper>
[00000000] false
[00000000] false
[00000000] nil
[00000000] step: WAKE_AT
[00000000] nil
[00000000] step: QUIESCENT
[00000000] true
[00000000] false
